# Private config source pointer (git-ignored).
#
# The public repo ships only committed templates for the operational data
# (etc/machines.ini, etc/reuter.ini, etc/team.ini). The real values live in a
# separate, access-controlled private repository — a small git repo whose
# tracked files are `machines.ini`, `reuter.ini` and `team.ini`, plus any
# consumer-specific dev-only files such as `hosts` (a hostname→IP mapping),
# mirroring the consumer's etc/. This file tells `fetch-private-data` where to
# find that repo.
#
# Copy this file to `.private-source` (untracked, machine-specific) and set
# the one retrieval mechanism — a git URL (+ optional ref), fetched on demand
# into var/private-data (git-ignored, like the rest of /var/):
#
# PRIVATE_DATA_GIT=git@example.com:team/app-private-config.git
# PRIVATE_DATA_REF=main
#
# The private repo's committed content is the single source of truth: no
# machine runs on a loose, uncommitted copy. `reuter.ini` is the only file
# that ever ships to a prod host (whole, via bin/deploy-private-config);
# `machines.ini` and `team.ini` stay dev/deploy-time and never leave the
# deploy machine. A `.private-source` that sets no PRIVATE_DATA_GIT is an
# error, not a silent skip; an absent `.private-source` is a no-op (prod has
# no git and no pointer — it resolves the stable per-app dir from
# DEPLOY_PRIVATE_CONFIG_DIR instead).
#
# This file never discloses the private repo's identity in the public history
# (it is git-ignored). Access control on the private repo is the real security
# boundary — see doc/system/private-config.md.
